 \begin{abstract}
-Single-GPU mixture-of-experts (MoE) inference can stall on HDD reads when expert weights exceed the HBM and host memory assigned to a task. Managing HBM and host residency independently can discard experts that later tokens reuse. We present MECC (MoE Expert Cache Compiler), which couples profile-guided initial placement with joint HBM/CPU retention and dependency-aware weight delivery. The compiler assigns per-layer capacities; the runtime updates resident experts from unchanged router selections. In single-run FP16 Mixtral-8x7B measurements on one A100, MECC with uniform initialization delivers decode speedups of 1.77$\times$ over local streaming and 2.95$\times$ over fixed-CPU retention, with matching sample perplexity.
+Single-GPU MoE inference reads expert weights from HDD when a task's HBM and host allocation cannot retain the model. Initial placement and later GPU evictions determine how often these reads recur. We present MECC, the MoE Expert Cache Compiler, to connect layer-wise initial allocation with joint HBM/CPU retention. The compiler emits capacities and ranked expert IDs. The runtime promotes selected experts and retains GPU victims on the host. Delivery orders reads and copies before consumption. In single-run FP16 Mixtral-8x7B measurements on one A100, MECC with uniform initialization takes 7.38~s per decode token. This gives 1.77$\times$ the streaming rate and 2.95$\times$ the fixed-CPU rate, with matching sample perplexity.
 \end{abstract}
 
